@page "/Projects/SiepieAndTakkieDialogSystem"

<div class="ProjectPage">
  <DefaultProjectHeading CurrentProjectURL="Projects/SiepieAndTakkieDialogSystem" />
  <div class="ProjectDescription">
    <div>
      <h3>Project description</h3>
      <span>
        The dialog system is made for the game<NavLink> Siepie and Takkie game</NavLink>. It loads dialog per scene
        based on the file name and location.
        If it has loaded dialog it can display it and manage the conversation flow.
      </span>
    </div>
    <h3>How to use it</h3>
    <span>
      In the scene where you want to use the dialog system attach the InitDialogSystem component to an empty GameObject.
      From then on every scene gets checked for a corresponding json file in the default path:
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/siepietakkiedialogsystem/DefaultFilePathDialogSystem.png" />
    <span>
      The InitDialogSystem instantly calls don't DontDestroyOnLoad(this.gameObject) in order for the per scene loading
      to work.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@DontDestroyOnLoad" />
    <span>
      This also means you should destroy it externally if you don't intend on keeping it around.
      As seen in the void Start() the InitDialogSystem subscribes to the SceneManager.activeSceneChanged in order to
      listen for scene changes.
      When the scene changes it gets the new path for the json and then sets it as the new path for the Deserializer.
      The Deserializer itself gets the actual json file through the Resources class.
      Then when GetDeserializedObject gets called it returns the generic with which the class was instantiated.
      In this case it's the DialogData object.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@DialogData" />
    <span>
      Make sure that the scene JSON is properly set up.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@UID" />
    <span>
      UID is the unique identifier that binds the dialog to the npc (in order to bind it you need to set the UID for the
      NpcDialogPreparer to the same number).
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@SequenceID" />
    <span>
      This is the identifier NpcDialogPreparer uses to iterate over the dialog.
      The order at which the dialog is placed in the JSON doesnt matter as long as you properly assign the order with
      this order.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@Speaker" />
    <span>
      The character that's actually saying the dialog should be put here.
      The keyword "PLAYER" gets the name of the character that interacted with the npc.
      The dialog property should be self explanatory. It's the dialog the characters will say.<br />
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@JsonExampleDialogData" />
    <span>
      DialogData is then used to feed the data to the NpcDialogPreparer.
      This class gets called when the NpcInteract receives an interaction request from the PlayerInteractor class.
      NpcDialogPreparer then continues to instantiate the DialogTemplatePrefab and keeps a reference to the
      UIDialogSequenceManager that's in the prefab.
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/siepietakkiedialogsystem/DialogTemplatePrefab.png" />
    <span>
      The UIDialogSequenceManager is the layer that directly controls the UI.
      It does so by Querying the UXML document for it's elements. It does this by searching for the Tags that I set up
      in the UXML document.
    </span>
    <img src="/images/projects/UXMLTags.png" />
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@UIDialogSequenceManagerQuery" />
    <span>Once all the references and instances are in place, NpcDialogPreparer keeps listening to NpcInteract in order
      to iterate through the DialogData.Lines
    </span>



    <span>This is the visual layout of the UXML document.</span>
    <img src="/wwwroot/images/projects/siepietakkiedialogsystem/UXMLVisual.png" <img
      src="/images/projects/SiepieAndTakkieDialogDummy.png" />

    <h3>The whole system also has full test coverage</h3>
    <span>Since I was completely new to the testing topic with no real clue as to how a unit test looked in the first
      place. I decided to spend some time looking at different testing frameworks since those seemed to get recommended
      for writing unit tests over and over again. So that arose two questions: Why use a testing framework? And what are
      the differences between the frameworks.</span>
    <h3>The actual tests I wrote</h3>
    <img style="width: 55%" src="/images/projects/TestLists.png" />
    <CodeBlock CodeString=@UnitTest />
    <span>For unit testing I decided to go with NUnit just because it’s already incorporated with unity’s testing
      framework. Without this integration, I wouldn’t have the testing UI in unity. So even though learning something
      newer and more flexible like xUnit might be more beneficial for other use cases, UTF (Unity testing framework) is
      completely integrated with NUnit.</span>
  </div>
</div>


@code {
  string UnitTest = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["UnitTest"];
  string DontDestroyOnLoad = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["InstantDontDestroyOnLoad"];
  string DialogData = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["DialogData"];
  string UIDialogSequenceManagerQuery = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["UIDSequenceManagerQuery"];
  string UID = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["UID"];
  string SequenceID = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["SequenceID"];
  string Speaker = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["Speaker"];
  string JsonExampleDialogData = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["JsonExampleDialogData"];
}